body:
Hrm, I toyed around with this idea, and ran into this problem:
```c++
void test() {
	Span<int> span = make_a_span();
	Span<int> span1 = span;  // invalid due to deleted copy constructor / assignment
        if (some_bool) {
            span1 = span.subspan(2, 10);  // could be made valid with move constructor
        }
        // Do stuff with span and span1
}
```

You may have an algorithm that uses a `Span` for the whole range, and later narrows it down on some condition. This would be normal behaviour we would accidentally prevent.

```suggestion
#include "core/typedefs.h"

#include <type_traits>
```

If you wanna get *really* fancy, you can make the length argument implicit:
```suggestion
	Span() = default;
	Span(T *p_ptr, size_t p_len) :
			_ptr(p_ptr), _len(p_len) {}
	template <size_t len>
	Span(T (&p_ptr)[len]) :
			_ptr(p_ptr), _len(len) {}
```

```
uint64_t _len = 0;
const T *_ptr = nullptr;
```
to have a memory layout similar to vector ( @RandomShaper  talked about it during the meeting)
